Required Hardware and Software
------------------------------

Use on various platforms
------------------------
Currently, at FNAL, TRANSPORT is being developed on a UNIX platform, and 
certified using a set of test files and files representing beamlines.
The Accelerator Division has used TRANSPORT on a VAX platform as well as
SUN workstations.  

To compile and link on a VAX, simply FOR and LINK the list of .for files 
transfered from the selected version sub-directory.

TRANSPORT has been built on an SGI as a test.  For version v1_0, there 
were several compiler and linker warnings.  A single test input deck was 
run through this executable, and the results matched the results 
obtained on a VAX for the same input, to within a reasonable level of 
precision.  That is, the results differed only in the 4th digit to the 
right of the decimal. The user is warned to make careful selection of 
the UNITS he tells TRANSPORT to work in, to be certain that differences 
in floating-point operations on various platforms do not affect the 
results.

   The Makefile used in this SGI test was  (-g -w to suppress warnings) 
FFLAGS = -g -w

OBJECTS = transport.o ranport.o trm.o trin.o trsec.o trcall.o

transport       : $(OBJECTS)
        $(F77) $(FLAGS) $(OBJECTS) -o $@

After v1.5, a single Makefile is used to build all flavors.
One can simply cd to the top directory of transport and issue

	make FLAVOR=<the operating system name (AIX, IRIX+6, IRIX46, etc)
or
	make	# the default FLAVOR is as supplied by 
		# ups flavor -2.


Product Installation
--------------------

Each version of TRANSPORT in this public area is placed in its own sub-
directory (v1_3, v1_4, v1_5, etc).  The subdirectory will contain all 
the source code needed to build the full program. This README file 
contains a list of the version sub-directories, their dates of creation, 
and what was changed in the source code.

TRANSPORT has traditionally been maintained in 5 pieces. The code is all 
in FORTRAN. The names of the five pieces and their functions are:

        TRCALL          Main calling routine
        TRANSPORT       Main steering routine and block data
        TRM             Main body of code
        TRIN            Code to handle translation of the input deck
        TRSEC           Code to handle higher order terms in magnets
        RANPORT         A random number generator used for simulation of
                          errors
      
      
Running the Software
--------------------
VERSION LIST and COMMENTS
-------------------------

v1_5
----
Initial public version, certified in 4/95. A predecessor of this version 
was used for the FNAL Main Injector design, and for that version some 
variables were changed from single to double precision.

This version uses standard input and output for its IO.  On a UNIX 
platform, one executes the program by redirecting the std io
        transport < in.dat > in.out
where in.dat is supplied by the user, following the input deck format as
outlined in the Transport manual.

On a VAX, one can assign filenames to standard input and output
        ASSIGN/USER in.dat FOR005
        ASSIGN/USER in.out FOR006

Following instructions in the manual, one can request binary output from
TRANSPORT for use in plotting and other applications.  These special 
output files will be named as standard fortran units, fort.11 on Unix, 
and for011.dat on a Vax.


